well let's speak now to dr. Hamid Khan  of the BMA he's here in the studio and  Rachael Clarke a junior doctor who joins  us live from Oxford good evening to you  both dr. Clark start with you the BMA  had recommended this as the best deal  junior doctors were going to get so why  vote against it it's very simple and  Jeremy Hunt in a way put it very  eloquently in his own words he's still  talking about his government so-called  manifesto pledge for a seven-day NHS  what he has fails to hear for an entire  year is our objection to the notion that  a seven-day NHS can be delivered with no  funding and no extra staffing the NHS is  already desperately underfunded and  frontline junior doctors like me are on  our knees pretty much every day that we  go into work that's even before he  attempts to stretch services even more  thinly over seven days there are no more  doctors he's not delivering any more  doctors he's not delivering any  resources this seven-day NHS was always  a fiction that he has used as a stick to  beat junior doctors like me and we have  said no over and over again the thing  none of us can understand now is why he  still refuses to listen Jerry Hunt has  pointed out though dr. Rachael Clarke  that only forty percent of those  eligible to vote rejected the contract a  third didn't vote at all what happens  now absolutely I mean I think it would  be a grave error to assume that the  doctors who did vote for the contracted  so as an endorsement of the contract I  am one of that food I voted for the  contract with a very heavy heart very  reluctantly because I saw it as a bad  option but the least worst option in the  new brexit climate that we face I think  what has to happen now and if Jeremy  Hunt has any concerns about patient  safety he would strive to do this he  needs to recognize and here our concerns  and he needs to address them he needs to  stop hiding behind rhetoric at the  moment there are gaps in all our rotors  he needs to address those he needs to  find ways of filling the gaps with  new doctors he means to recognize that  currently doctors do not receive  adequate training they have no whistle  blowing protection whatsoever in the NHS  they're the only group of employees in  the NHS that have that lacked that  protection and he needs to address that  what he has to stop doing is spinning  actions speak louder than words and he  needs to start acting in a way that can  rebuild the corrosive and devastating  effect he has had on the trussell wrong  doc doesn't dr. Clark are we going to  see further strike action I think that's  a very real possibility I think right  now the stakes are high and there is a  window in which the Department of Health  and Jeremy Hunt by her actions could do  something to address this situation he  used to pretend this entire dispute was  the making of the BMA that somehow we  had been whipped up into a frenzy by our  militant union well the vote today  proves that that is is not accurate this  is an opposition Eric's bring in dr.  Hamid car on on that point about the BMA  I mean there are those who say that the  poisonous atmosphere this is all down to  the poisonous atmosphere that has  emerged in this very bitter dispute that  whatever Jeremy Hunt was going to say  wasn't going to be accepted does the  beer may bear some responsibility for  that no I don't think it does to the  contrary the BMA has tried its best to  come up with a solution in a very  difficult situation the Health Secretary  has deliberately toxify the atmosphere  around these talks he has turned this  into a bitter confrontational dispute  and it never had to be he did so by  calling the BMA radicalized by using the  term militant for doctors even spoke  about nuclear options and at worst he  even associated the Paris attacks and  tried to scare the public by suggesting  that we wouldn't work if there was a  terrorist attack here if we were on  strike and it is the BMA didn't do any  stirring up at this debate you're saying  not at all not at all we have simply  tried to present the facts as best as we  could and if anything Johann marijuana  has tried as best to make things easier  for the government by giving them this  opportunities now gone so what happens  now what does this do for the  ability of the BMA who's going to be  there to negotiate this contract again  well that's very simple the BMA has  procedures there will be a new head of  the junior doctors committee and then  they will take negotiations forward but  that is the wrong premise the question  that we should be asking is how the  health secretary will change his  approach or whether the government will  have a new health secretary in place we  have a situation what this is about do  you think do you think this is about the  political uncertainty that we're  currently undergoing and that people  think junior doctors may think that  there may be a new health secretary  there may be an opportunity to get more  no I don't think this has been  opportunism at all the state of affairs  are such that we have the one of the  lowest levels of funding in the NHS out  of all developed countries the lowest in  the g7 group of countries we have a  health secretary who's deliberately  stoked up confrontation with the terms  that I just said and the fundamental  premise of this contract the seven-day  NHS is based on completely manipulated  flawed evidence to the extent that even  the authors of the report of the paper  rather which Jeremy Hunt keeps citing  have rubbished it they've said that it's  completely untrue so we need to get rid  of that premise we need to get rid of  this obsession with a so-called  seven-day NHS which is completely  undefined which is completely  unconscious ound bites dr. rachel clark  me Jeremy Hunt gave quite a lot of  ground in this dispute didn't he I mean  there were major concessions made on pay  and on working conditions and on  protections for people having to take  time out for example and are you  concerned that you could end up with a  worse deal I really don't see it in  those terms I'm afraid I don't think he  did concede very much at all and I think  that this vote demonstrates that the  contract we have on the table currently  is sexist it discriminates against women  it offers no whistleblower protections  junior doctors it does very little to  address the rotor gaps that are plaguing  every hospital in the country at the  moment and crucially at no stage  he ever acknowledged the fact that if he  really wants to deliver safer weed  cancer patient something that he and his  prime minister have claimed over and  over again the barrier to doing that is  not asked junior doctors it is the lack  of funds from central government and  until he addresses those fundamental  points he leaves junior doctors like me  feeling demoralized undermines cheapened  and humiliated by this dispute and i'm  afraid the first and most important act  he could do to rebuild this situation  would be to do the honorable thing and  resign okay dr. rachel clark and dr.  Hamid Khan thank you both very much  indeed
 

